body:
Schema

The schema declares named text fields and metadata fields. This is its required top-level shape, illustrated with an example schema:

```ts
{
  text: {
    title: { boost: 2 },
    body: { boost: 1 }
  },
  metadata: {
    kind: { type: 'string', multi: false, nullable: false },
    tags: { type: 'string', multi: true, nullable: false },
    year: { type: 'number', multi: false, nullable: true }
  }
}
```

These are schema data, not implementation code. Defaults are `boost: 1`, `multi: false`, and

Record

```ts
{
  id: 'guide:storage#recovery',
  text: {
    title: 'Recover a saved collection',
    body: ['First paragraph.', 'Second paragraph.']
  },
  metadata: {
    kind: 'guide',
    tags: ['storage', 'recovery'],
    year: null
  }
}
```

The only top-level keys are `id`, `text`, and `metadata`. `text` is required and may be empty; `metadata` is optional. Reject unknown field names. A declared text field may be missing, a string, or an array of strings. `null`, `undefined`, mixed arrays, and

Explanation facts

Core exposes opt-in typed score facts to the Inspector capability. Normal searches must not allocate full explanation trees. Selected-contribution facts include:

- revision, model, analyzer identity/profile, document ID, canonical query/group identity;
- membership outcomes and which positive groups participated;
- field name, boost, winning normalized term/variant, and variant weight;
- `tf`, `df`, `N`, document field length, total field length, and average length;
- `k1`, `b`, IDF,

